id:TASK-260728-1g0z69
name:define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract
status:done
blockedBy:["TASK-260728-2kp3tv"]
description:Define the closed manifest toolchain requirement, trusted resolution, version comparison and installation-guidance contract shared by all compiled drivers.
scope:Driver-to-toolchain mapping; exact versus bounded version constraints and prerelease policy; OS and architecture applicability; trusted executable resolution and fingerprinting; two-stage preflight with host availability/version before source acquisition and go.mod, rust-toolchain.toml, swift-tools-version or selected Kotlin metadata cross-check after validation/audit but before compiler work; typed missing, incompatible, untrusted and mismatch diagnostics; manager-owned guidance IDs and catalog lifecycle; no auto-install.
ac:The reviewed decision is canonical and implementation-ready, has deterministic cross-language version semantics, prevents package-selected paths, URLs, channels, commands and trust roots, defines cache and receipt identity effects, defines both fail-fast stages without bypassing source audit, and maps official platform guidance without embedding stale package-controlled instructions.
notes:Execution base: continue from accepted curator-spec candidate /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-2kp3tv/curator-spec-worktree and create a task-owned worktree/copy; preserve that predecessor read-only and do not stage, commit, publish, advance release pins, or claim platform validation. Define one closed v1 contract shared by local vendored and external-repository builds for Go, Rust, Swift, and Kotlin: manifest declares manager-defined toolchain id plus an explicit version constraint/compatibility requirement; manager policy alone resolves trusted executable paths and primary-source/platform remediation guidance. Package/repository data must not supply executable paths, download URLs, channels, package-manager/install commands, environment overrides, or trust roots. Host availability/version preflight must run before source acquisition or persistent mutation; source metadata compatibility cross-check runs only after local source validation or exact external acquisition+audit and before compiler work. Missing/unparseable/incompatible toolchain is an installation error with stable diagnostics and manager-owned recommendations; v1 never auto-installs. Resolved executable identity/version/target fingerprint must bind cache, receipt, marker/currentness semantics without changing frozen released artifacts. Include normative ordering, schemas/registry, positive+negative vectors, deterministic generation and release gates; keep skill-build.json as the sole neutral external descriptor.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-af7289, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-af7289)\nDelivered decision 0007 (canonical) plus docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md (implementation-ready reference) and one CHANGELOG bullet, in task worktree /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/curator-spec-worktree. Predecessor TASK-260728-2kp3tv worktree preserved read-only (still 127 dirty entries at 57c1f56). Nothing staged, committed, published, pinned; no platform validation claimed.\n\nContract: toolchain-registry-v1 maps each driver to one primary toolchain plus companions over the closed id set go/rust/swift/kotlin/jdk; go entry complete, rust/swift/kotlin/jdk reserved with a fixed obligation list their own driver decisions must fill on a qualified host. toolchain-requirement-v1 is exactly {id, version} with exact/at_least/range over a canonical major.minor.patch triple; combination is interval intersection (associative, commutative, source-order-independent) and an empty intersection is rejected before the host is probed. Prereleases satisfy nothing and cannot appear in a requirement, because no cross-language prerelease order exists. Resolution admits only manager-bundled or trusted operator config; the forbidden-origin and package-influence lists are exhaustive. Two-stage preflight: Stage A (availability/version/platform) right after manifest parse, before external acquisition, cache lookup and persistent mutation; Stage B (go.mod / Cargo.toml + rust-toolchain.toml / swift-tools-version / selected Kotlin field) after local validation or exact external acquisition and audit, before any cache candidate read or compiler child. Metadata is an assertion, never a selector. Eleven stable build_toolchain_* codes carry a guidance_id only; toolchain-guidance-catalog-v1 is manager-owned, versioned, total (release-gate enforced), primary-source-only, text+URL never a command. No auto-install in v1, named explicitly for rustup/swiftly/sdkman/asdf/mise/Homebrew/winget/Gradle wrapper/GOTOOLCHAIN.\n\nKey load-bearing property: a version constraint can only filter the manager-trusted set, never add a candidate. Effective requirement is a gate, not a build input (same reasoning that excluded capability evidence in decision 0006); no bypass because Stage A precedes cache lookup. Result: zero frozen artifacts change and adopting the contract causes no rebuild. Go costs no new process - Stage A reuses the three existing package-independent bootstrap probes, so the five Go argument-vector forms stay five and manager-worker-v1 is untouched.\n\nStated asymmetry: a descriptor requirement is unreadable at Stage A because the descriptor arrives with the repository, so Stage A gates on baseline INTERSECT manifest and the descriptor requirement joins at Stage B. That is why the consuming-manifest requirement is REQUIRED in the next schema.\n\nGates, standalone, real exit codes - validate 0 (42 schemas / 422 vector files, before and after), python unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0, generate-vectors 0. Regenerated conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json are byte-identical to the accepted predecessor. Clean git-init probe: regenerate-check x2 = 0, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 = 0. Two negative probes expected-red and red: retired descriptor stem in the new doc rejected by validate (exit 1), broken local link in decision 0007 rejected by validate (exit 1); both reverted, final validate 0. Note: make regenerate-check cannot pass inside the task worktree because it diffs against 57c1f56 while the rc.5 candidate is uncommitted - determinism proven by predecessor byte-comparison and the clean probe instead.\n\nDeliberately not done: no protocol/profile/SECURITY/schema/vector change (TASK-260728-2jaw7h), no schema version numbers (TASK-260728-2spy93), no README release-table row (would falsely claim the design is part of the rc.5 specification set), and no invented probe argv or normalization for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk (needs a qualified host).\n\nReviewer focus: is the filter-never-select claim airtight across manifest, descriptor and every stage-B metadata source; is Stage A movement genuinely gate-neutral; is excluding the effective requirement from the cache key correct given Stage A precedes lookup; are the reserved obligation lists complete enough that downstream driver decisions cannot reopen selection; does prerelease rejection break a case worth admitting.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-af7289, pid=8002, exit=0)\nReviewer focus: treat this as the research/design contract whose normative schema/vector landing is explicitly TASK-260728-2jaw7h. Independently challenge whether constraints can only filter manager-trusted candidates; Stage A ordering is truly before acquisition/cache/mutation without dropping required predecessors; Stage B cannot bypass source validation/audit; descriptor asymmetry is fail-closed; prerelease/version intersection is deterministic; package data cannot select path/URL/channel/install/env/trust; guidance is manager-owned primary-source text/URL only and v1 cannot auto-install. Verify whether excluding the effective requirement and guidance catalog from cache identity is sound while resolved toolchain identities remain bound, and whether reserved Rust/Swift/Kotlin/JDK obligations are sufficient for downstream work. Re-run docs/link/retired-name and unchanged rc.5 release gates; no stage, commit, publish, pin advance, or platform claim.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-e30a89, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-e30a89)\nReviewer verdict: changes requested and routed to analysis. Evidence is attached as TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict.md. Blocking issues: preserve the existing manager-tested Go release-family allowlist; make guidance coverage total for all eleven diagnostics; make guidance IDs compatible with supersession; remove the package-channel diagnostic contradiction; and correct the cache vector so current incompatibility fails before lookup rather than rebuilding.\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-e30a89, pid=13554, exit=0)\nRework cycle 1, blocking reviewer findings: (1) preserve the existing Go manager-tested-release-family gate by adding a manager-owned closed compatibility set/predicate to each registry entry, evaluating it in Stage A with deterministic diagnostic/guidance and preserve/reject vectors; at_least alone must not admit unknown Go 1.99. (2) Make guidance total over all 11 diagnostics with an explicit code-to-reason mapping, including requirement_invalid, requirement_unsatisfiable, version_undetermined, package_influence_forbidden, and changed. (3) Make guidance_id lifecycle implementable by adding immutable revision/generation or otherwise defining distinct valid IDs and superseded_by semantics. (4) Resolve channel contradiction: distinguish forbidden package-selected install/toolchain channels from source metadata channel assertions, define precedence and deterministic positive/negative vectors. (5) Correct vector 35: incompatible current resolved toolchain fails before cache lookup; only a compatible current toolchain with a different cached toolchain identity causes cache miss/rebuild. Update decision, reference and evidence, rerun all unchanged-rc.5/links/negative/release gates, and hand off for another independent review. Preserve the task worktree and do not stage, commit, publish, pin, or claim platform validation.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-e41666, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-e41666)\nRework cycle 1 complete: all five blocking reviewer findings addressed in the same task worktree /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/curator-spec-worktree. Delta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly decisions/0007-compiled-build-toolchain-preflight.md, docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md, one CHANGELOG bullet. Nothing staged, committed, published, pinned; no platform validation claimed. Predecessor TASK-260728-2kp3tv worktree preserved read-only (127 dirty entries at 57c1f56, nothing staged).\n\n(1) Go family gate preserved. New REQUIRED registry field compatibility: a closed manager-owned set of tested release families matched by exact set membership, never ordering. go = {(1,23)}, carrying profiles/manager.md 2.2 forward unchanged. Independent of the requirement - a constraint can neither widen nor narrow it and nothing on the wire surface reaches it. New twelfth diagnostic build_toolchain_untested_release carrying the admitted family set. Stage A gains step 7 with fixed precedence: Go 1.22.0 -> incompatible (step 6), Go 1.99.0 -> untested_release (step 7), so at_least alone admits nothing. Vectors 7 preserve, 31 reject, 32 precedence, 57 identical cache key.\n\n(2) Guidance totality mechanical. reason is exactly the diagnostic code suffix, so the code-to-reason mapping is the identity and stays total as codes are added; the five uncovered reasons plus untested_release are covered. New guidance_class (host/configuration/authoring) fixes the admissible primary_source origin per class, which is what makes authoring and configuration reasons carriable at all - the language official origin, the manager operator documentation, or this specification, all manager-trusted. Vectors 47, 48, 53.\n\n(3) Guidance lifecycle implementable. Grammar is toolchain.<id>.<reason>.<platform>.r<N>, N decimal starting at 1, strictly increasing per tuple. Published IDs immutable; a change of meaning/origin/class creates the next revision; superseded_by must name an existing same-tuple entry with a strictly greater revision, so the chain is acyclic; exactly one active entry per tuple; retired entries retained. Selection still resolves the tuple. Vectors 49, 50, 51, 52.\n\n(4) Channel contradiction resolved by surface, not by field type. New reference 3.1: on the manager-defined wire surface the field set is closed, no channel field exists and any such value is forbidden; on the source-ecosystem metadata surface each entry declares a closed disposition table (forbidden/compared/ignored) where forbidden is reserved for values naming WHERE a toolchain comes from and channel/track values are compared. Fixed precedence: forbidden fields evaluated before compared, Unicode-scalar order of source path then field path, so path plus nightly is always package_influence_forbidden. Channel classification is deterministic: version literal = at_least assertion; stable = permitted and never honored (deliberate relaxation - it asserts exactly the release-only property v1 enforces); beta/nightly/dated = mismatch; anything else = mismatch. Stage B classification vectors take the resolved version as fixture input, so reserved-toolchain cases need no host. Vectors 10, 11, 35-42.\n\n(5) Cache vector corrected. Old vector 35 removed. Reference 4.3 and decision section 6 now state two distinct rows: an incompatible current toolchain fails at Stage A with cache lookup never reached, no candidate consulted, no rebuild, no mutation; a cache candidate carrying a different toolchain identity under a compatible current toolchain is an ordinary key miss and rebuild. Replaced by positive 14 and negative 45.\n\nVector inventory 40 -> 58 cases. Decision gained five rejected alternatives (requirement-only admissibility, package-reachable family set, blanket channel prohibition, tuple-only guidance IDs, host-only guidance reasons) and states that no currently admitted Go release becomes inadmissible and none currently rejected becomes admissible.\n\nGates, standalone, real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas / 422 vector files), python unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0, generate-vectors 0. Regenerated conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical to the accepted predecessor. Clean git-init probe at 1b02fa0: regenerate-check x2 = 0, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 = 0. Two negative probes against the reworked files expected-red and red: retired descriptor stem rejected by validate (exit 1), broken local link rejected by validate (exit 1); both reverted, final validate 0. make regenerate-check still cannot pass inside the task worktree because it diffs against 57c1f56 with the rc.5 candidate uncommitted - determinism proven by predecessor byte-comparison and the clean probe instead. Python gates run with the existing validation-venv because ambient python3 lacks jsonschema.\n\nReviewer focus for this cycle: does compatibility fully restore manager.md 2.2 and is requirement-before-compatibility the right precedence; is the 3.1 surface split a real distinction rather than a relabeling; is channel=stable permitted rather than mismatched correct (the one place this cycle relaxes previous text); do the identity code-to-reason mapping and guidance_class keep primary_source honest for authoring/configuration reasons; is the revisioned ID plus one-active-per-tuple sufficient for TASK-260728-ypbuav; does splitting old vector 35 into positive 14 and negative 45 leave any cache-versus-fail-fast case unstated.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-e41666, pid=17140, exit=0)\nReview cycle 2 focus: independently verify each prior blocking finding is closed, not merely restated. In particular, Go 1.99 must fail the manager-owned exact tested-family compatibility predicate despite satisfying at_least; pre-baseline precedence must be deterministic; all 12 diagnostics must have a total valid guidance mapping; revisioned guidance IDs must permit immutable supersession and one active tuple; invalid declared ids must still resolve guidance safely; manifest/descriptor channels remain forbidden while source metadata compared fields can never select/activate a toolchain and forbidden fields win; current incompatibility never reaches cache while differing compatible toolchain identity is a miss/rebuild. Challenge whether stable source channel acceptance and guidance primary-source classes preserve trust. Re-run unchanged rc.5, deterministic, link, retired-name and release gates. No edits, stage, commit, publish, pin or platform claim by reviewer.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-092e80, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-092e80)\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260728-092e80, pid=29455, exit=0)\nREWORK CYCLE 2 directive 2026-07-28: queued for the first free Claude Opus 5 producer slot; continue only in the existing task-owned curator-spec worktree and preserve the accepted predecessor read-only. Close all four cycle-2 blockers from TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-2.md: (1) make the complete Go metadata classifier exhaustive over every officially admitted ToolchainName class, including toolchain default, canonical release names, and custom/non-canonical names, with deterministic outcomes and focused vectors; (2) partition or explicitly order overlapping wire invalid-vs-package-influence and Stage-A unavailable-vs-untrusted triggers, with overlap vectors; (3) define a representable typed diagnostic payload union/optionality for invalid and empty-intersection requirements, including retained validated source fragments and payload-shape vectors; (4) define allowed guidance coverage modes, exact-OS plus any fallback behavior, and append-only/cross-version retirement transitions, with hybrid and transition vectors. Preserve the twelve-code totality, tested-family gate, revisioned IDs, source-metadata disposition split, cache ordering, all rc.5 bytes, and the previous five accepted fixes. No schema/release/pin/commit/staging change; rerun deterministic and unchanged-rc.5 gates, attach a new rework artifact, and hand off to a new independent reviewer cycle.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-1ccd8b, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-1ccd8b)\nRework cycle 2 complete: all four cycle-2 blocking findings closed in the same task worktree /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/curator-spec-worktree. Delta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly decisions/0007-compiled-build-toolchain-preflight.md, docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md and one CHANGELOG bullet. Nothing staged, committed, published, pinned; no platform validation claimed. Predecessor worktree preserved read-only.\n\n(1) Go classifier exhaustive. New reference 4.2.1-4.2.4. One shared goVersionLiteral grammar yielding a base triple plus prerelease flag. The go directive has 4 total classes and NO forbidden class - a value with a path